---
// /trust — product\10 §6: vier Punkte, die kein größerer Anbieter glaubwürdig kopiert.
// Kein Vergleich mit größeren Anbietern (R1) — die LAGE wird beschrieben, kein Name genannt.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ReifegradChip from '../components/ReifegradChip.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Trust"
  description="What stays on your machine, what leaves it, and why: local-only trading data, a licence check you can verify, and the stop-loss that stays with your broker no matter what happens to us."
  narrow
>
  <h1>Why hand a one-person company your trading?</h1>
  <p style="color: var(--muted); font-size: 1.08rem;">
    That question is fair, and the honest answer isn't a promise — it's an architecture you
    can check. Your tool is tied to an account that belongs to someone else; that's true no
    matter who built it. Here's what changes when almost nothing leaves your machine.
  </p>

  <h2>What stays on your machine, what leaves it</h2>
  <p>
    Nearly everything FixedR does runs locally: broker connections, market data, the
    application itself, your journal, your account configuration, every trade it places.
    Exactly two things ever reach a server we operate, and nothing else does.
  </p>
  <div class="grid" style="margin: 1.4rem 0 2rem;">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Stays on your machine</h3>
      <p>Broker connections, market data, order execution, your journal, account
        configuration and trading history — the whole working system.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Leaves the machine</h3>
      <p>Only two things: a licence/subscription check, and the allocation step that decides
        account order and per-account risk when a trade goes to several accounts at once.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    Neither of those two carries a price, a symbol or a trade — only what a licence check
    needs, and the account IDs and risk percentages an allocation decision needs.
    <ReifegradChip id="local-only" /> The server never learns what you trade.
  </p>

  <h2>The sunset clause</h2>
  <!-- TODO Michael: exakter Sunset-Wortlaut, sobald FXR-28 gebaut ist und/oder in den AGB
       steht (product\10 §6 Punkt 2 / §10 Frage 1: Zusage erst nach AGB, dann Seite). -->
  <p>
    <ReifegradChip id="sunset-clause" /> The intent, not yet the exact terms: if the signing
    key behind the licence check ever goes silent for an extended stretch, licence enforcement
    is meant to lapse for good — the tool keeps working, the subscription requirement doesn't
    survive us. The precise wording isn't settled yet, so we're not going to phrase it here
    before it is.
  </p>

  <h2>Export, on request</h2>
  <p>
    <ReifegradChip id="data-export" /> A committed direction, not yet built: your journal and
    trading records should leave in an open format whenever you ask, not only while you keep
    paying.
  </p>

  <h2>The stop-loss lives with your broker</h2>
  <p>
    <ReifegradChip id="stop-loss-at-broker" /> FixedR places the stop-loss as an order at your
    broker, the same way any manual order would sit there. If our software stops running, if
    our server disappears, if the machine you run FixedR on loses power — the stop is already
    resting where the broker enforces it, not waiting on us to still be there.
  </p>

  <section class="callout" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
    <p>
      Trust is an architecture question first. What FixedR does with that architecture is on
      <a href="/product">Product</a>. <a href="/waitlist">Join the waitlist →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
